# Design Notes – LearnTrack

## Why ArrayList Instead of Array
ArrayList is used instead of arrays because it provides dynamic resizing.
In this application, the number of students, courses, and enrollments is not fixed
and can grow at runtime.

ArrayList also provides built-in methods such as add(), remove(), and iteration support,
making the code cleaner and easier to maintain compared to plain arrays.

---

## Where Static Members Were Used and Why
Static members are used in the IdGenerator utility class.
The ID counters and ID generation methods are static to ensure that:
- IDs are unique across the entire application
- There is a single shared counter for students, courses, and enrollments
- No object creation is required for ID generation

This is a correct use of static members because ID generation does not depend on object state.

---

## Where Inheritance Was Used and What Was Gained
Inheritance is implemented using a base class called Person.
The Student class extends the Person class and reuses common fields such as id, firstName,
lastName, and email.

This approach reduces code duplication and improves reusability.
It also demonstrates polymorphism by allowing method overriding
(e.g., customizing the getDisplayName() method in the Student class).

---

## Clean Code Practices Followed
- Methods are kept short and focused on a single responsibility
- Method names are meaningful and descriptive (e.g., addStudent, findStudentById)
- Business logic is separated from UI code
- Entity classes contain only data and getters/setters
- Service classes handle application logic
- The Main class handles only menu display and user input
